{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-335646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-335639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335639" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-335470" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335470" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335470" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-333360" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-333360" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-333360" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-333360" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-333360" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-333360" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Solutions approach the equilibrium only along specific trajectories."] ["text" "Solutions converge to the equilibrium only if the system is linear."] ["text" "Solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it."] ["text" "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$"] ["text" "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "All"] ["text" "Some"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Some"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "b12ebbeabbf60b0d65b270cb9dd83a733db77282371b1317f5c16955f2970304"} {"question" "q-335460" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget"