"e-21665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21665.json" "file_sha256" "a6913b5f1ad1f454fcf7c4244ca05b7a166777f9e1b3e63d791ddb9e6994809e"} {"question" "e-21665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/assets/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21665.json" "file_sha256" "a6913b5f1ad1f454fcf7c4244ca05b7a166777f9e1b3e63d791ddb9e6994809e"} {"question" "e-21666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n- Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21666.json" "file_sha256" "6c293d74cb2e4a164182899e80e96891dc2f7b0f62079a12532760f37ab808a3"} {"question" "e-21666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21666.json" "file_sha256" "6c293d74cb2e4a164182899e80e96891dc2f7b0f62079a12532760f37ab808a3"}] "usage" {"e-21665" [] "e-21666" [] "q-333385" [] "q-333360" []} "basis" 2344 "retractions" [[17592186221896 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - y(t) - 1$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [17592186221896 :question/answer-fields 17592186220316] [17592186221115 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = y^{2}(t) - x(t)$\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above."] [17592186221115 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 0; y^{2} - x = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 2y = 0⇒y = \\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{2})^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0$, we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0$.\n- For $x=4$, we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186221116 :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n1. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n2. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n3. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n4. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$."] [17592186221116 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O$, approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty$.In plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$, remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0$.In plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it's not stable.In plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\n> Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220317 17592186220318 17592186220319 17592186220320 17592186220321 17592186220337 17592186220338 17592186220339 17592186220340 17592186220341] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220316 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220316 :answer-field/id] [17592186220316 :answer-field/key] [17592186220316 :answer-field/type] [17592186220336 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220336 :answer-field/id] [17592186220336 :answer-field/key] [17592186220336 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
